speechtext:
Thank you, Mr. Speaker. So, to start again:
1.	That she had accompanied Leonard Peltier to the Jumping Bull Hall in Oglala, South Dakota, during the first week of May, 1975;
2.	That about one week after their arrival, Peltier and a number of other individuals started to plot the murder of all officers of the U.S. Bureau of Indian Affairs or the FBI that might come to the area;
3.	That Peltier appeared to be leading the plot;
4.	That certain responsibilities were allotted and a detailed escape route established;
5.	That, the day before the murder of the FBI officers, Peltier told her the FBI officers would be in the area to serve an arrest warrant and she should prepare to escape after the expected shots took place;
6.	That she saw Leonard Peltier fire on men she believed to be FBI officers; and
7.	That she saw Peltier again in August, that they referred to the murder of officers and that he told her he had lost his head, had opened fire and shot at them.
The affidavits by Myrtle Poor Bear were not the only evidence introduced during the extradition hearings. Other evidence was submitted confirming Mr. Peltier's guilt, which lead the judge to conclude there was sufficient evidence against Mr. Peltier. The evidence included the gun issued to the murdered FBI officer, Jack Coler, that he carried on duty and which Peltier had in his possession. They included the bullets of a Colt A.R.-15 rifle found in his car and in another vehicle on which Peltier's fingerprints were taken. The Colt A.R.-15 rifle was similar to that used in the murder of both FBI agents. Finally according to the testimony of two eyewitnesses, Peltier had fled the scene of the crime with such a weapon.
Adjournment Debate
On the basis of those pieces of evidence, Justice Schultz concluded in a long written decision that the conclusive evidence warranted the extradition of Peltier on two charges of murder. Moreover, he concluded that other pieces of evidence were sufficient to allow him to include in the extradition order another murder charge and one of a burglary. Nobody has ever suggested that the extradition based on those last two charges were irregular.
Mr. Peltier submitted to the Federal Court of Appeal under Clause 28 of the Federal Court Act a request that the decision rendered by Justice Schultz be repealed. The hearing before the Federal Court of Appeal was held in October, 1976. Previously, Mr. Peltier's lawyer had made an application to obtain the permission to introduce another piece of evidence. That piece of evidence was a third affidavit. This was in fact the first affidavit made by Myrtle Poor Bear on February 19, 1976 which had not been introduced during the extradition hearing held before Justice Schultz in Vancouver. In that affidavit, Myrtle Poor Bear said that she had left the area where the Jumping Bull hall was located the day before the two FBI agents had been shot and had never returned there.
